# Docker build-context exclusions.
#
# What the build actually consumes (must NOT appear below):
#   - builder stage `COPY . .`  -> Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock, src/**
#   - runtime stage             -> hermes-plugin/** (setup.py + ams_memory/),
#                                  docker/entrypoint.sh
# Verified safe: no build.rs and no git-metadata crates in Cargo.lock (so .git
# is unneeded); src/** contains no include_str!/include_bytes! pointing at the
# excluded dirs or root markdown files (only env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION")).

# Rust build products: rebuilt from scratch inside the image; the local target/
# is a Windows dev build that could never be reused for the Linux target and
# only bloats (and platform-pollutes) the context.
target

# Embedding models are downloaded at container start by the entrypoint
# (`asuna-memory model-download`), never at build time.
models

# VCS + IDE/scanner/agent caches: irrelevant to cargo build and the wheel.
.git
.scannerwork
.claude
.claw
.ace-tool
.vscode

# CI/workflow definitions and human-facing docs: the image build reads none of
# them. (README* patterns are single-segment, so they only match the root
# README.md / README_ZH.md — hermes-plugin/README.md stays in
# the context; Cargo.toml's `readme` key is metadata that `cargo build` never
# opens.)
.github
docs
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
HISTORY.md
HISTORY_ZH.md
for_ai.md
README*.md

# Local SQLite databases and WAL/SHM sidecars (root-level only; src/** and
# hermes-plugin/** contain no *.db files needed by the build).
*.db
*.db-*

# Python test cache inside the plugin; `pip wheel` reads only setup.py +
# find_packages(), so pytest's own state is excluded with it.
hermes-plugin/.pytest_cache

# Stale local setuptools artifacts (gitignored, so CI checkouts never contain
# them, but a local `docker build` would copy them in): `pip wheel
# --no-build-isolation` reuses build/lib and never removes orphans, so an
# outdated build/lib could silently ship removed/renamed modules in the wheel.
hermes-plugin/build
hermes-plugin/*.egg-info
**/__pycache__
